      "summaryMarkdown": "S1 meets R1.C1, R1.C2 and R3.C2 with no wire or behavior regression, and all four findings are verified resolved against snapshot `5932d0a4…`.\n\n- **R1.C1:** 34 stable ids over the real codec and storage, with a faithful mirror of the non-stateful receive and send paths.\n- **R1.C2:** identical, warmed-up, release-only, three-round interleaved workloads against the official client, with complete results.\n- **R3.C2:** generation-tagged, payload-free sequencing on both targets, including wire-level rejections and resend outcomes, asserted by 8 `sequence_trace_` tests.\n\nNotes for later sprints:\n- **S3, measurements:** the warmed run puts Rust `send-throughput` at about 0.07x of .NET (30k vs 440k sends/s), far below R2.C4's 0.95x.\n- **S3, benchmark source:** when hot-path changes begin, extract one dispatch function shared by `run_receiver` and `bench_support` so baselines keep measuring the receiver.\n- **S3, baseline paths:** Criterion stores ids under `target/criterion/hot_paths_inbound_json/...` (slashes become underscores).",
