      "descriptionMarkdown": "**Expected:** R1.C2's identical workloads measure both clients in a comparable state, because R2.C4 turns these numbers into budgets against the official client.\n\n**Observed (original):** every round starts a fresh process, the Rust binary is ahead-of-time compiled, and the .NET reference was timed largely on tier-0 JIT code and first-use initialization: `stream-throughput`, `send-throughput` and `fan-in` had no warm-up, and `invoke-latency`'s warm-up lasted about 4-40 ms.\n\n**Repair (requested):** amend R1.C2/D1 for an identical, untimed, steady-state warm-up of every workload on the measured connection(s) in both clients, record its size, document it, keep .NET's default tiering, and rerun `s1-perf-harness`.\n\n**Verified resolved:** R1.C2/D1 now require the warm-up (2 s full / 0.2 s quick, where every minimum count is a tenth). `tests/perf/main.rs` and the PerfReference `Program.cs` implement the same loops with identical scale constants on the same measured connection(s), and the csproj and runner leave tiering at its defaults. Every result records `warmup_ms` and its warm-up count, and `ExpectedMetrics` requires them. `RunOnceAsync` refuses non-release results before anything is recorded (D1 invariant). The warmed quick runs confirm the bias was real and is gone: .NET fan-in 11.5k to 26.5k calls/s, send-throughput 100k to 440k sends/s, MessagePack stream 87k to 438k items/s, while the Rust client moved at most 1.5x.",
      "boundaryId": "S1"
    },
    {
      "id": "S1-F2",
      "criterionIds": [
        "R3.C2"
      ],
      "severity": "minor",
      "status": "resolved",
      "regressionRefs": [
